example, if I can get T3 and T4, but since I already have a Chi of T3-4-5, I don't have those tiles. So playing T2 is safe.\n\nBut the question is, which move is best. Let's recap:\n\n- If I play J2, J1, or J3: safe, as they are not part of any melds. Discarding them allows me to keep working on B melds.\n\n- If I play B7: risk losing the chance to form a B Chow or Pung.\n\n- If I play B8: risk losing the B8 needed for the B Chow.\n\n- If I play B6: same as B8.\n\n- Play T5: I have two T5s. If I discard one, maybe someone can use it. But the T5 is part of the Chi. Since the Chi is already formed, discarding a T5 might be okay. But if I have two T5s, maybe I can form a Pung if I get another. The unshown tiles have T5:3, so there are three left. If I discard a T5, someone else might pick it up, but it's not helpful for me. So playing T5 is possible, but not ideal.\n\n- Play T2: as discussed, not part of any melds.\n\nSo the best options are to play one of the J tiles. Between J1, J2, J3. Given that the player across has played J2, maybe playing J2 is less likely to be useful to them. Alternatively, if I play J3, which is the most common in the unshown tiles (J3:4), it's safe.\n\nBut I need to choose one. The answer should be one of the valid actions. Let's pick Play J2. Because the player across has already played J2, so they might have another one. If I play J2, they can Peng it, but that's their problem. For me, it's safe to discard a J2 to reduce my hand and keep working on the B melds.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [Discarding J2 allows forming a B6-B7-B8 Chow if B7 is retained, and avoids blocking potential melds.]\nReason: [Keeping B7s for a potential Pung or B6-B7-B8 Chow is strategic. Discarding J2, which is less critical for melds, maintains flexibility.]\nAnswer: Play J2"}
